Tinea Corporis
Common Infections and Afflictions
Annular scaly plaques, Central clearing, Advancing scaly border

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CIA-042
Reason for Visit: Rapidly spreading, itchy circular rash on the torso.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Patient triaged via telephone reporting a rapidly expanding, intensely pruritic, and "scaly" rash on the torso that has appeared over the last 48 hours. The sudden, extensive nature of the eruption has caused significant patient distress and anxiety regarding a potential highly contagious or systemic illness. An urgent home assessment is required to perform a definitive dermatological evaluation, rule out serious inflammatory conditions like Pityriasis Rosea or Secondary Syphilis, and initiate immediate antifungal therapy to arrest transmission. Delaying this assessment poses an unacceptable risk of household spread and escalating patient panic, satisfying the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I have these red, itchy circles spreading all over my stomach and back, and they are getting bigger by the hour.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 3 days ago as a small red patch, which has since evolved into multiple pruritic and annular (ring-shaped) erythematous plaques. The patient notes a distinct "central clearing" within the older lesions and an advancing, raised, and scaly border. The distribution is asymmetric on the trunk and extremities. There has been a gradual, outward centrifugal spread. The patient notes the itch is worsened by warm and humid environments. History of contact with a new pet (kitten) two weeks ago. Denies systemic symptoms such as fever, weight loss, or joint pain.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living with family; concerned about spreading the rash to children.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/78 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Visibly anxious and scratching the affected areas.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. A positive KOH preparation showing segmented hyphae would assist in confirming the diagnosis, but clinical presentation is characteristic.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination reveals multiple annular, erythematous plaques ranging from 2cm to 5cm in diameter across the abdomen and left upper arm. Lesions feature a prominent, raised, and scaly leading edge with noticeable central clearing. There is an absolute absence of deeper dermal nodules, vesicles, or pustules. The distribution is markedly asymmetric. No oral or genital mucosal involvement noted.
Environmental Observations: Kitten present in the home; no other obvious hygiene concerns.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has applied a generic moisturizing cream with no improvement.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Tinea Corporis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Pityriasis Rosea - Ruled out by the asymmetric distribution, the presence of distinct annular "rings" with central clearing, and the lack of a characteristic "Christmas tree" pattern.
Eczema (Nummular) - Unlikely given the lack of chronic atopic history and the presence of a raised, scaly leading edge which is pathognomonic for fungal infection.
Granuloma Annulare - Ruled out by the presence of scale; Granuloma Annulare typically presents with smooth, non-scaly annular borders.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Potential for household transmission)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The explosive appearance of multiple, itchy, ring-shaped lesions caused significant patient alarm and warranted prompt clinical differentiation from aggressive inflammatory or infectious dermatoses. Home assessment rapidly confirmed a classic presentation of Tinea Corporis (Ringworm), likely zoonotic from a new pet. Given the rapid spread and the patient's role in a multi-person household, immediate initiation of targeted topical antifungal therapy and strict infection control education were required to arrest the spread. This timely intervention and containment definitively satisfy the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS 585-600 assessment.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Terbinafine 1% cream to be applied to the affected areas and a 2cm margin of normal skin twice daily for 2 weeks. Advised on the need for veterinary review of the kitten.
Positive Behavioral Change: To definitively stop the fungus from spreading to your family, use a dedicated towel for the affected area only, and immediately place it in a hot wash after every single use. The fungus lives in the tiny skin flakes on the towel; physically isolating the laundry prevents the microscopic spores from traveling to other family members.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: None.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 1 week if the rash is not clearly receding.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Seek medical review if the rash begins to develop painful, fluid-filled blisters, if the skin becomes hot and intensely painful, or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending clinical response; fungal infections require persistent treatment.
Clinical Course: Itch should improve within 48-72 hours of starting treatment. The redness and scaling will take 10-14 days to clear. Treatment must continue for one week after the skin appears normal to prevent recurrence.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate review if the rash spreads to the scalp or if large, boggy, painful lumps develop within the rash.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: This is a superficial fungal infection of the skin. The cream works by physically destroying the cell walls of the fungus, but it can only do so if it is applied consistently to the "leading edge" where the fungus is most active.
Actionable Steps: Keep the skin dry, as moisture encourages fungal growth. Wear loose cotton clothing and avoid sharing personal items like hairbrushes or towels.

